title: vLLM/SGLang/LlamaCpp read no env var for an --api-key-protected server
updated: 2026-08-17T05:03:01Z
---

Follow-up from karr #93, which introduced the three-state credential contract
(api_key_env names the variable actually read, api_key_required says whether it is
mandatory).

Ollama and the LM Studio family now sit in the "optional" state: they read
LANGERTHA_OLLAMA_API_KEY / LANGERTHA_LMSTUDIO_API_KEY and work without it.

vLLM, SGLang and LlamaCpp do NOT read any environment variable. They inherit
Role::OpenAICompatible (api_key attribute, _build_api_key { undef }, Bearer header sent
only when defined), so a user CAN authenticate, but only by passing api_key => ...
explicitly in code. All three servers can be started with a token
(`vllm serve --api-key ...`, `python -m sglang.launch_server --api-key ...`,
`llama-server --api-key ...`), and then every client must send it.

api_key_env { undef } is therefore truthful today, but the optional state is now
expressible, so the ergonomic gap is worth closing: add _build_api_key reading
LANGERTHA_VLLM_API_KEY / LANGERTHA_SGLANG_API_KEY / LANGERTHA_LLAMACPP_API_KEY and drop
the api_key_env override (derivation yields exactly those names), leaving
api_key_required { 0 }.

This is a behaviour change (an exported variable would start being picked up), not a
semantics fix, which is why #93 deliberately left it out. Whisper is a separate case:
_build_api_key returns the dummy string "whisper" and no env var is plausible.
